"Psalms":{"t":"How Psalms Is Built","p":["- Book I (1–41) · Nearly all David. Mostly cries for help from a man under pressure, with trust breaking through.","- Book II (42–72) · The sons of Korah, then David again. Longing, confession and the king, closing with “the prayers of David son of Jesse are ended.”","- Book III (73–89) · Asaph and the sons of Korah. Hard questions: the temple in ruins, and in Psalm 89 the promise to David’s line seems broken.","- Book IV (90–106) · Opens with Moses. The answer to Book III: kings may fail, but the LORD reigns.","- Book V (107–150) · Gathered home. Thanks, pilgrim songs on the road to Jerusalem, and a rising flood of praise.","From cries to praise: cries for help crowd the first half of the book. Praise takes over toward the end, until the last five psalms are nothing but Hallelujah.","“God” or “the LORD”: in Psalms 42–83 the writers mostly say “God” (Elohim) where the rest of the book says “the LORD” (Yahweh). Psalm 53 even repeats Psalm 14 with the name changed.","Closing blessings: each of the five books ends with a short blessing of the LORD (41, 72, 89, 106), and Psalm 150 closes the whole book in praise. Many readers see an echo of the five books of Moses.","The big arc, as many readers see it: God’s King is crowned (2), the kingdom falls (89), the people learn again that the LORD reigns (93–100), and they come home singing (107–150)."]}
